<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gediz River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hermus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gediz, the ancient Hermus, is a river of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> that flows about 400 kilometres from the highlands near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutahya">Kütahya</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> north of İzmir. Its fertile plain was the heartland of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> on its tributary the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pactolus">Pactolus</a>, and later of the Saruhanid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manisa">Manisa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gediz-river.jpg" alt="Gediz River" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mr. Necdet Düzen · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GedizRiver_IzmirProvince_Turkey.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
